olossus seems  straightforward you raise your magical  sword to the Sun follow its beam of  light and kill the Colossus resting at  that location each of the beasts dark  energy then snakes out from its corpse  and into wanders body warping him back  to the starting trine exploding the  stone idols that line the wall doorman  hints at the location of the next  Colossus and you're off to repeat the  same process of course this is the most  reductive possible explanation of the  game I haven't mentioned many things  first off these colossi are the only  enemies in the game despite being an  open-world the Forbidden Lands aren't  lined with filler content on your way to  the first boss it feels weirdly quiet on  your way to the second it feels  downright eerie there are no NPCs  waypoints or side quests aside from  upgrades in the form of stamina boosting  lizards and health improving fruit there  are no other collectibles to speak of in  2005 this minimalist approach made  colossus an anomaly the recent grand  theft auto san andreas and mercenaries  playground of destruction relied on  worlds packed to the brim with things to  do people to kill and missions to  complete the upcoming Elder Scrolls for  oblivion perhaps more than any other  game to date was built on this idea  today developers have spent more than a  decade making open-world titles  littering them with objective markers  and quest icons AI companions and  pointless collectables we played so many  of them that we forgot what made open  worlds great to begin with in 2005  Shadow the Colossus felt like a warning  today it feels almost like a lament  games used to be open-world for a reason  aside from a few standout titles many  have lost sight of them in 2017 The  Legend of Zelda breath of the wild  released and much like Shadow of the  Colossus reminded me why I love  open-world games it captures the pure  joy of exploration and uses its open  environment as a core design principle  its sprawling environment isn't tacked  on as an additional feature but it fuels  its every mechanic  [Music]  [Applause]  the similarities between shadow the  Colossus and Zelda don't end there  from Ito await a renowned creative  director from team eco said that  Colossus was his riff on a Zelda game in  an interview with insert credit in 2005  who ate a set of the game's colossi  they're not bosses they're more like  inverted Zelda dungeons after only the  first giant you kill you see what he  means it's not just about identifying  the creatures weak spots and striking  him where it hurts but also figuring out  how to get to those spots there's often  environmental puzzle solving - as you  expose this Giants weak stomach or  distract this one from one angle before  assaulting it from another you start to  realize that these colossi don't just  fill the traditional role of the boss  fight but also the trials you face to  get to that boss fight instead of locked  doors and enemies in your way there is  only the Goliath and anything between  your sword and it's vulnerable organs  permeating all of this is a melancholic  soundtrack  the music swells with sad crescendos  during Colossus battles amplifying as  the creatures fall to the ground with  their final whimpers although many of  the colossi are aggressive it's hard not  to feel guilty as they collapse they  have the animations of humans or  domestic pets in the final moments  before their death and the game makes  sure to send them off with a somber  soundtrack of course the guilt you feel  upon killing one Colossus is nothing  compared to what's coming if you've been  Shadow of the Colossus you know what I'm  talking about if you haven't I'll give  one more spoiler warning and suggest you  go play it now come back and finish this  video Colossus is known for having one  of the better endings to a video game  for a reason the entirety of Shadow of  the Colossus draws on myths legends and  fables the theme of resurrection and the  p