ic this serpent-like sand creature  requires mastery of archery horse riding  stamina management and spatial awareness  in order to take down but more than that  if you pay attention it hints at what's  really going on because it doesn't once  fight back even as you're stabbing it  beneath its stony dorsal fins weakening  it to the point of exhaustion it keeps  just wandering the desert  that's all it wants to do several other  colossi follow suit while some of them  are aggressive once you enter their turf  others won't attack until provoked  you're coming into their home and  killing them for reasons you don't fully  understand because you never bothered to  question what you're doing there are a  handful of other games that use an end  game twists making you question your  actions up to that point I won't spoil  them out of the blue here but you can  probably think of one major 2007 title  that does something pretty similar but  even so I think Shadow the Colossus has  one of the more affecting stories of any  game I've played it conveys guilt regret  and sorrow emotions that I don't seek  out in everyday life but they're  powerful nonetheless that a videogame  could make me genuinely sorry for the  things I had killed was at the time  almost unheard of today it's etched into  my memory if the act of replaying shadow  the Colossus on ps4 isn't enough to  cement it as a masterpiece in your mind  look to other games in recent years  destiny bosses require little more than  a restless trigger finger Ubisoft has  given us open-world games with content  for contents sake for the better part of  a decade even the very recent Monster  Hunter World a game I love and I'm  playing at every single chance I get  encourages us to hunt creatures kill  them  and then just wear them as trophies much  like it was a reaction to other video  games in 2005 Shadow the Colossus still  feels monumental today and with its  recent remake I'm hoping more people pay  attention from here on out  [Music]  you

